In article <AACC104C@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>,
Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com> wrote:
> Whenever something seems "impossible", I like to develop a wild theory that 
> might explain it, just to keep me from being to smug about what is and isn't 
> "possible".  As an exercise, here is what I thought up concerning astrology:
> 
> First of all, two of the "planets" of astrology, the sun and moon, do actually 
> have measureable effects on earth in the form of tides and seasons.  The tides 
> can be predicted by moon positions and/or phases, and the seasons can be 
> predicted by the sun's apparent progression through the zodiac.  Thus, some 
> "planetary" bodies do actually influence our environment here on Earth.
> 
> Could there be more subtle, harder to explain, influences?  One's sun-sign is a 
> function of the time of year of birth.  One's time of year of birth determines 
> one's time of first conscious memories (assuming an average developmental time 
> between birth and first memories).  The time of one's first memories will also 
> determine the season of the year outside.  One's first conscious memories of the 
> world may be icy and cold, sunny and green, hot and muggy, grey and rainy, brown 
> and dying, or full of holidays and vacations depending on when you were born.  
> 
> Could these earliest perceptions of the outside world influence one's later 
> beliefs and attitudes about the world in general?  I have migrated this question 
> from a mystical one to a psychological one.  It may be that one's earliest 
> memories influence basic personality traits, and thus, knowing one's sun-sign 
> might have some predictive value in determining one's personality traits.
> 
> A wild theory, to be sure, but not "impossible".  Therefore I hesitate to say 
> that it is "impossible" for astrology to predict anything about my personality.
