From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Mon, 5 Dec 1994 13:27:24 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-12-153] FICTION: Aristoi (Spoilers!)

Philip Goetz wrote:
>I just finished _Aristoi_ by Walter Jon Williams, a science fiction story
>concerned with many of the same ideas as Extropians, such as nanotech,
>life extension, cognitive enhancement (though it takes an Eastern approach
>to this), galactic expansion, and how to manage a social system in
>which some people have godlike powers.

I think the cognitive enchancement scheme was very interesting. Most
people in this world utilizes specialized sub-personalities ("daemons")
for multitasking (using an implanted computer to run most of them to avoid
interference with normal mental processes). I think the author may be on
to something here, I see some similarities to descriptions of
semi-independent elementals/thought forms in ceremonial magick and the
psychology of split personalities. Although the implanted computer would 
probably be needed to avoid interference (and to expand their 
capabilities), daemons might be a possibility. They would of course 
require some quite extensive metaprogramming, but the multitasking and 
multiple viewpoints would probably be worth it.


>The first thing I asked as I read this book was, "Why are people pretty
>much the same as today when this takes place 1500 years in the future?
>Why is their society so static?"  There is a reason: the Logarchy is the
>ruling class, and their "prime directive" is to prevent their human charges
>from suffering.  They have FTL travel, but limit expansion in the Universe
>by the number of Aristoi (aristocrats) that there are to rule over new
>worlds.

I found that this society raised some interesting questions. While I 
seriously doubt that the Aristoi system would work (it assumes that all 
Aristoi are responsible and rational), I found the idea of different 
domains with different political and cultural systems interesting. I have 
always been suspicious of the idea that there is a perfect political system. 
Instead it might be a good idea to have many different systems running in 
paralell, hopefully with as little friction as possible (in the Logarchy 
citizens have the right to move between the different domains, making 
some domains attract certain people). After all, its better to have a 
plethora of different systems than a single system, which may collapse 
due to some unexpected problem. 

The society in Aristoi seems to have voluntarily avoided the grand 
Singularity, seeing that it would be very dangerous for mankind (probably 
true) after Earth was eaten by Gray Goo. The system seems to have 
developed just after this disaster, and after they had stabilized 
things, the Aristoi created a very stable society to prevent any further 
catastrophes. 


>SPOILER
>Only, I thought the bad guys were doing basically the right thing, though
>perhaps they should have started further back in evolutionary history.
>I think the author intended some ambivalence.

I agree, one of the main themes was the fight between uniformity (or at 
least limited form) and diversity (at any cost). Neither side was exactly 
the good guys. Personally I would have tried with many different ways of 
seeding planets, possibly using self-replicating probes as mentioned at 
the end. Its noteworthy that the villains in some way succeeded, since 
they forced the Logarchy to start expanding against its will.

