From: Mark Fischer <M_FISCHER@ACM.ORG>
Date: Mon, 05 Dec 1994 09:36:43 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-12-157] ECO

> On 12/3/94 at 2:57 AM, betsys@cs.umb.edu wrote:

> b>  Presumably, Yellowstone is worth more as a long-term investment as
> b> a tourist attraction than it would be as a source of oil.

William_John@gnj.or.jp (William John) responded:
> That's a pretty big 'presumably,' though, isn't it?  And if the people who have
> control of Yellowstone decide to cash in now on the oil (or strip-mining, or
> whatever), it'll be gone pretty much forever.  No bringing it back after that
> happens.  Doesn't seem like a very secure way to manage our wilderness.

Exactly. That is why some of us have a problem with purely market driven
management of natural resources. It all sounds great in theory, but
sometimes the "market" does not correct itself until AFTER a few species
have been removed from the planet, forever. Or until an entire region has
been deforested. The whole "logging creates jobs" argument for continued
cutting of old-growth forest was absurd. Just ask anyone who _used_ to
log on the Olympia peninsula; there aren't many jobs when there are no
trees left. Likewise for fishermen in the Maritime Provinces of Canada;
not many jobs when there are no fish left. Simply put, human societies
tend to be _extremely_ short-sighted. If someone can postulate a
sound market-based mechanism that successfully rewards long-term thinking,
this would not be such a problem.

Further, humans do not always act on "market forces". People drove
the Carrier Pigeon into extinction for the sheer "sport" of it. Likewise
shooting Bison from the windows of passing trains was done for "fun",
leaving the corpses to rot where they fell. What "market forces" were
in play then? Humans are capable of killing because they think it
is "fun", not because they are hungry or cold. Until people can
demonstrate that we've learned, or evolved, to act more responsibly,
there will be fuel for the passions of Earth First! types.

-mf

