From: Mark Fischer <M_FISCHER@ACM.ORG>
Date: Mon, 05 Dec 1994 10:08:19 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-12-158] upgrading

I am curious. With all the talk of uploading, enhanced abilities,
etc., any discussion seems to presume these new abilities can be
managed. Considering that the human brain is a hack, (With 3 distinct
layers, reptilian, mammalian, and the most recently evolved,
consciousness providing layer), and considering that the lower two
regions are an abundant source of irrational behavior (that are
tolerated, even expressed in the upper); has anyone pondered the
stability of this structure were it to be upgraded?

	I am (obviously) not a neurobiologist. But there seem to be
Lots of problems with the existing architecture. Humans even today
are a lot like Intel boxes running Windows being asked to
run a multi-tasking, multi-user, multi-media application with
full-motion video. NONE of the system was ever designed for the
task; what you want is an SGI box; ie hardware/software that was
designed for the task at hand.

  It simply seems to beg credulity to think we can slap some
add-on memory, neurons with a faster clock, maybe some sensors
for the other parts of the E-M spectrum, etc. on to the existing
architecture, and expect _anything_ that is actually stable from
the result. More likely, we'll end up with lots of "unexplained
application error"'s.

	So, the simple question is: is anybody aware of work/research/essays
being done in this area?

	-mf

