From: William_John@gnj.or.jp (William John)
Date: Mon,  5 Dec 94 16:33:22 +0900
Subject: [#94-12-160] The Machinery Of Friedman

On 12/4/94 at 5:32 PM, johnkc@well.sf.ca.us wrote:

j> He would be tried by an independent arbitrator picked by
j> BOTH sides. The arbitrator would be paid by the case not by the
j> year, those who gained a reputation for fairness would get a lot
j> of business ( remember both sides picked him) those who favored
j> one side or made stupid or brutal decisions would not be picked
j> again and be looking for a new line of work.

The problem with this, it seems to me, is the same one I see with the many plans
and ideas advanced to further extend market incentives and consumer choice into
our medical care system.  People are just too busy to make well-informed
decisions about what hospital to go to for brain surgery, or what arbitrator to
go to for a decision about a violated business contract.  In addition, when
these kind of situations arise (a brain tumor or a legal dispute), quick action
is usually desirable, and time and money spent informing oneself about and
choosing a judge or a doctor could be time and money that one could ill afford. 


In other words, choosing a doctor or judge is much different from choosing the
cheapest or best brand of macaroni and cheese at the supermarket, and I don't
think we can expect the same principles to apply.

